WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.663
How do detections get built?

2
00:00:01.663 --> 00:00:02.588
As code.

3
00:00:02.588 --> 00:00:05.314
The rule library lives in version control,

4
00:00:05.314 --> 00:00:10.300
and nobody edits a production rule in the
portal; a change is a reviewed commit.

5
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.662
Every rule has one named owner,
a test and a review date.

6
00:00:14.662 --> 00:00:17.100
Each rule moves through five steps.

7
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.850
Author it, starting from Microsoft's
template where one exists.

8
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.298
Test it against history and
replay a known-bad sample,

9
00:00:24.298 --> 00:00:27.825
because a rule that can't be
shown to fire isn't finished.

10
00:00:27.825 --> 00:00:30.185
Run it alert-only for at least two weeks,

11
00:00:30.185 --> 00:00:32.987
and until its false
positives are documented.

12
00:00:32.987 --> 00:00:33.850
Promote it.

13
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.750
Then review it at least quarterly.

14
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.200
Where a vendor analytic isn't available
in the agency's cloud, the program

15
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.575
rebuilds an equivalent inside its own
boundary, and says it isn't parity.

16
00:00:44.575 --> 00:00:45.637
Plan for rot.

17
00:00:45.637 --> 00:00:48.778
Thresholds drift, sources
stop without an error,

18
00:00:48.778 --> 00:00:54.038
and Sentinel disables a rule after repeated
failures and marks it auto disabled.

19
00:00:54.038 --> 00:00:57.890
The standing tuning effort is the
real cost of having a library.
